"l4_ch1_title" = "Leopold's cellar";
"l4_ch1_intro" = "The cellar in Mühlgasse holds Leopold's bench and an empty shelf; customers wait on the corner. Jonas Brandt, who used to water the plants here, says: \"First the crate, then the money. And don't keep the van waiting.\"";
"l4_ch1_outro" = "The cellar runs without you. Brandt waters, Varga drives, and on the corner nobody asks for Leopold any more, only for you. Sami Okafor knocks: she knows the Old Town, and the Old Town knows her.";
"l4_ch2_title" = "The Old Town";
"l4_ch2_intro" = "In the Old Town you don't sell on a corner, you sell to order. Sami has contacts at the Old Market and the university, and a notebook full of people who pay more when it's quick. Loud pays better and is remembered.";
"l4_ch2_outro" = "The Old Town takes what you deliver. Ilse Morgen, who kept Leopold's books, puts an invoice in front of you: special orders pay more from now on, because she makes the numbers prettier. Dario Feld is waiting at the harbour.";
"l4_ch3_title" = "The harbour";
"l4_ch3_intro" = "At the harbour it's tons, not baggies. Crystal for Quay 7 and the works: bulk, heavy, hungry for fleet space. From now on your goods share the same van, and you decide who goes first.";
"l4_ch3_outro" = "The harbour swallowed its first bulk order without a cough. Viktor Sand keeps the quay quiet: attention fades faster from now on. Greta Lind calls from the Night Quarter.";
"l4_ch4_title" = "The Night Quarter";
"l4_ch4_intro" = "Nobody in the Night Quarter buys on a Tuesday lunchtime. But when club night starts, everyone wants it at once. Pills need stock: fill the shelf before the music starts.";
"l4_ch4_outro" = "Club night sold out, the toilets were full, the police were elsewhere. Greta stays and makes every night a minute longer. In the Upper Town a lawyer with very clean gloves is waiting.";
"l4_ch5_title" = "The Upper Town";
"l4_ch5_intro" = "In the Upper Town it isn't how much you deliver but whether you do. The villa and the Senators' Club pay for coke in small amounts and for punctuality. Every order on time makes you more trusted; every one missed reminds them who you are.";
"l4_ch5_outro" = "The Upper Town greets you by your first name now. Anton Kraus takes the conversations nobody has on the telephone. Mira Kessler says it is time for the whole city.";
"l4_ch6_title" = "Varnholm";
"l4_ch6_intro" = "All of Varnholm orders from you now, and everything at once. City orders want every good you make, and they make noise in every quarter. At the end a contract lies on the table that Leopold never signed.";
"l4_ch6_outro" = "You sign. The rain doesn't stop, Varnholm doesn't stop, but the ledger is yours now, every line of it. Leopold would have laughed.";
